    "difficulty": 2,
    "difficultyReason": "Scientific/methodological argument requiring the test-taker to identify which answer confirms a load-bearing assumption (that the control and treatment populations were comparable). Trap (C) introduces an alternative cause; trap (E) strengthens a neighboring claim about treatment design rather than the conclusion.",
    "stimulus": "Researchers studying recovery from stroke gave one group of patients a daily regimen of physical therapy combined with a newly developed motor-stimulation drug, while a second group received physical therapy alone. After six months, patients in the combined-treatment group showed significantly greater restoration of limb function than those in the physical-therapy-only group. The researchers concluded that the motor-stimulation drug accelerated neurological recovery from stroke.",
    "questionStem": "Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The motor-stimulation drug was originally developed to treat a neurological condition unrelated to stroke."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Patients in both groups had suffered strokes of comparable severity and had received no prior physical rehabilitation."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Patients in the combined-treatment group received their physical therapy sessions earlier in the morning than patients in the other group."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Several of the researchers who designed the study had previously published research on motor-stimulation therapies."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Physical therapy alone has been shown to produce measurable improvements in limb function in the majority of stroke patients."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanations": {
      "correct": "The argument assumes the two groups were comparable at baseline — if the drug group happened to have milder strokes or prior rehab, the drug might get credit for a difference it didn't cause. Confirming equivalent stroke severity and no prior rehabilitation rules out this confound and directly supports the causal conclusion.",
      "A": "Accurate but irrelevant — the drug's developmental history neither rules out an alternative explanation nor bridges any premise-to-conclusion gap.",
      "B": "Correct. Confirms that the two groups were comparable, ruling out stroke severity and prior rehabilitation as alternative explanations for the observed difference.",
      "C": "Introduces an alternative cause — a timing difference in therapy sessions could independently explain the better outcomes, which weakens rather than strengthens the conclusion.",
      "D": "Irrelevant — researcher credentials speak to expertise, not to whether the drug caused the improvement.",
      "E": "Strengthens a neighboring claim (physical therapy works), but not the conclusion that the drug provided the additional benefit. Classic 'strengthens a different conclusion' trap."
    },
    "tags": ["strengthen", "causal_argument", "alternative_explanation_ruled_out", "medicine"]
  },
  {
