    "difficultyReason": "Economic/policy argument where the correct answer confirms a mechanism (that the tax relief actually reaches small businesses rather than being absorbed elsewhere). Trap (A) is topical but irrelevant; trap (D) strengthens the general desirability of tax relief without addressing the specific causal claim.",
    "stimulus": "A regional government recently reduced payroll taxes on businesses with fewer than twenty employees, reasoning that lower labor costs would allow small businesses to hire additional workers. Within eighteen months, employment at firms qualifying for the tax reduction rose by nearly twelve percent, while employment at larger firms not covered by the policy grew by only two percent. The government concluded that the payroll tax reduction caused the employment growth at small firms.",
    "questionStem": "Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Other regional governments have enacted similar payroll tax reductions for small businesses over the past decade."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "During the eighteen-month period, the region experienced no unusual economic expansion in industries dominated by small businesses.",
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Many of the small businesses that hired additional workers reported that the tax savings were their primary motivation for expanding their workforce."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Economists broadly agree that reducing the cost of labor encourages employers to hire more workers."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "The largest firms in the region employ, in total, more workers than all small firms combined."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanations": {
      "correct": "The argument's key inferential gap is whether the tax reduction was the actual driver of hiring or whether some other factor coincidentally boosted small-business employment. Businesses themselves reporting the tax savings as their primary motivation for hiring directly confirms the causal mechanism and rules out a coincidental explanation.",
      "A": "Unwarranted comparison — what other governments did neither confirms the mechanism in this region nor rules out an alternative explanation. Classic 'accurate but irrelevant' trap.",
      "B": "This rules out a confounding sector-specific boom, which helps, but (C) more directly confirms the causal link by providing evidence of the mechanism from the actors themselves. (B) is a partial strengthener but not the most direct support.",
      "C": "Correct. Confirms the mechanism by showing the businesses themselves attribute their hiring decisions to the tax savings.",
      "D": "Strengthens the general economic principle, not the specific conclusion that this policy caused this employment growth — 'strengthens a different (theoretical) conclusion' trap.",
      "E": "Irrelevant to whether the tax reduction caused small-firm employment growth; raw employment totals do not speak to causation."
    },
    "tags": ["strengthen", "causal_argument", "confirm_mechanism", "economics", "policy"]
  },
  {
    "id": "lr-gen-strengthen-d2-b9-03",
    "section": "LR",
    "subtype": "strengthen",
