    "difficultyReason": "Environmental science argument requiring identification of the answer that closes an analogical/methodological gap (whether the proxy measure is reliable). Trap (B) introduces a rival explanation; trap (E) is topically relevant but addresses a different part of the causal chain.",
    "stimulus": "Ecologists studying a coastal wetland measured the density of aquatic invertebrates — organisms known to serve as a primary food source for migratory shorebirds — at sites throughout the wetland. They found that invertebrate density was significantly lower at sites adjacent to agricultural runoff channels than at reference sites unaffected by runoff. The ecologists concluded that agricultural runoff is reducing the wetland's capacity to support migratory shorebird populations.",
    "questionStem": "Which one of the following, if true, provides the most support for the ecologists' argument?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Shorebird populations in coastal wetlands are currently declining faster than those in inland freshwater habitats."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Abnormally low rainfall during the study period caused water levels near the agricultural channels to drop, making those sites less hospitable to invertebrates.",
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Studies of comparable wetlands have found that shorebird abundance closely tracks aquatic invertebrate density across different sites within a wetland.",
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "The agricultural operations near the wetland use fertilizers that have been shown to alter the chemical composition of waterways."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Migratory shorebirds typically visit the wetland during a narrow seasonal window that coincides with peak invertebrate availability."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanations": {
      "correct": "The ecologists measured invertebrate density as a proxy for the wetland's ability to support shorebirds, but the argument requires that lower invertebrate density actually translates to reduced shorebird support. Showing that shorebird abundance closely tracks invertebrate density in comparable wetlands confirms this load-bearing assumption and closes the inferential gap between the proxy measure and the conclusion.",
      "A": "Introduces a comparison the argument doesn't make — inland vs. coastal trends do not bear on whether agricultural runoff in this particular wetland is reducing its shorebird-support capacity. Classic 'unwarranted comparison' trap.",
      "B": "Introduces an alternative explanation (low rainfall) for the lower invertebrate density near runoff channels, which weakens rather than strengthens the argument. Classic disguised-weakener trap.",
      "C": "Correct. Confirms the mechanism linking invertebrate density to shorebird support, bridging the key inferential gap in the argument.",
      "D": "Supports a premise (runoff has chemical effects) but does not connect those chemical effects to reduced invertebrate density or to reduced shorebird capacity — it strengthens an intermediate step without reaching the conclusion.",
      "E": "Accurate but irrelevant — that birds visit during peak invertebrate season does not speak to whether reduced invertebrate density at runoff sites diminishes the wetland's overall capacity to support those birds."
    },
    "tags": ["strengthen", "confirm_mechanism", "close_inferential_gap", "environmental_science"]
  }
